<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0150/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0150";
        dc:date                "2006-09-26 18:12:43.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFSN0150";
        pav:authoredOn         "2006-09-26 18:12:43.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0150/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_potential_energy_content_of_atmosphere_layer_due_to_advection/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/tendency_of_potential_energy_content_of_atmosphere_layer_due_to_advection>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The specification of a physical process by the phrase due_to_process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. 'tendency_of_X' means derivative of X with respect to time. 'Content' indicates a quantity per unit area. 'Layer' means any layer with upper and lower boundaries that have constant values in some vertical coordinate. There must be a vertical coordinate variable indicating the extent of the layer(s). If the layers are model layers, the vertical coordinate can be model_level_number, but it is recommended to specify a physical coordinate (in a scalar or auxiliary coordinate variable) as well. Potential energy is the sum of the gravitational potential energy relative to the geoid and the centripetal potential energy. (The geopotential is the specific potential energy.)"@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFSN0150";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_potential_energy_content_of_atmosphere_layer_due_to_advection"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UFAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/ZVTMSND1/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::ZVTMSND1";
        dc:date                "2023-04-24 17:39:19.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::ZVTMSND1";
        pav:authoredOn         "2023-04-24 17:39:19.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/ZVTMSND1/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/minimum_mass_ratio_of_water_to_dry_soil_for_soil_viscous_flow/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/minimum_mass_ratio_of_water_to_dry_soil_for_soil_viscous_flow>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"ratio_of_X_to_Y\" means X/Y. It may be expressed as a fraction, a percentage, or any other dimensionless representation of a fraction. It is the lower limit of the water content at which a soil sample will flow in a viscous manner. Known as the liquid limit."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::ZVTMSND1";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "minimum_mass_ratio_of_water_to_dry_soil_for_soil_viscous_flow"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UUUU/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N269/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N269";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N269";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N269/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mass_concentration_of_hexachlorobiphenyl_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mass_concentration_of_hexachlorobiphenyl_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/HLAT/>;
        skos:definition        "Mass concentration means mass per unit volume and is used in the construction mass_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y.  A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as 'nitrogen' or a phrase such as 'nox_expressed_as_nitrogen'. The chemical formula for hexachlorobiphenyl is C12H4Cl6.  This structure of this species consists of two linked benzene rings, each of which is additionally bonded to three chlorine atoms."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N269";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mass_concentration_of_hexachlorobiphenyl_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UKMC/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/T85RGWNF/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::T85RGWNF";
        dc:date                "2025-05-13 08:28:42.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::T85RGWNF";
        pav:authoredOn         "2025-05-13 08:28:42.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/T85RGWNF/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/sea_floor_horizontal_stress_due_to_model_sea_water_velocity/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "Ocean processes such as waves and currents can generate forces (or stress) on the sea floor. The surface called \"sea floor\" means the lower boundary of the ocean. \"Horizontal\" refers to the local horizontal, i.e. perpendicular to the local gravity vector at the location in which the quantity is calculated. \"Stress\" means the shear stress (force per unit area) exerted at the sea floor. Horizontal stress refers to the stress in the horizontal plane. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. The phrase \"model_sea_water_velocity\" means the stress associated with ocean current dissipation processes through bottom friction. This standard name is intended for the output of models in which sea surface waves are not included in the velocity field. Hence the sea floor stress due to bottom friction excludes any contribution from the dissipation of sea surface waves. If that contribution to the sea floor stress is separately modelled, it can be given the standard name of sea_floor_horizontal_stress_due_to_sea_surface_waves."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::T85RGWNF";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "sea_floor_horizontal_stress_due_to_model_sea_water_velocity"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UNSM/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/W2YRLZ9N/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::W2YRLZ9N";
        dc:date                "2023-10-16 17:23:15.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::W2YRLZ9N";
        pav:authoredOn         "2023-10-16 17:23:15.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/W2YRLZ9N/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/eastward_sea_water_velocity_due_to_ekman_drift/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/eastward_sea_water_velocity_due_to_ekman_drift>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "A velocity is a vector quantity. \"Eastward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed eastward (negative westward).The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::W2YRLZ9N";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "eastward_sea_water_velocity_due_to_ekman_drift"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UVAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CZCXUVJ2/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CZCXUVJ2";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CZCXUVJ2";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CZCXUVJ2/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/radioactivity_concentration_of_249Cm_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/radioactivity_concentration_of_249Cm_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Radioactivity\" means the number of radioactive decays of a material per second. \"Radioactivity concentration\" means radioactivity per unit volume of the medium. \"Cm\" means the element \"curium\" and \"249Cm\" is the isotope \"curium-249\" with a half-life of 4.43e-02 days."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CZCXUVJ2";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "radioactivity_concentration_of_249Cm_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/BQM3/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0777/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0777";
        dc:date                "2017-07-24 14:36:59.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFSN0777";
        pav:authoredOn         "2017-07-24 14:36:59.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0777/3/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0777/2/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0777/1/>;
        pav:version            "3";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/sea_surface_height_above_reference_ellipsoid> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/sea_surface_height_above_reference_ellipsoid/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "3";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/H021/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/H021/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/ASLV/>;
        skos:definition        "\"Height_above_X\" means the vertical distance above the named surface X. \"Sea surface height\" is a time-varying quantity. A reference ellipsoid is a regular mathematical figure that approximates the irregular shape of the geoid. A number of reference ellipsoids are defined for use in the field of geodesy. The geoid is a surface of constant geopotential with which mean sea level would coincide if the ocean were at rest. To specify which reference ellipsoid is being used, a grid_mapping variable should be attached to the data variable as described in Chapter 5.6 of the CF Convention. The standard name for the height of the sea surface above the geoid is sea_surface_height_above_geoid. The standard name for the height of the sea surface above mean sea level is sea_surface_height_above_mean_sea_level."@en;
        skos:narrower          <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G829/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/H026/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G836/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G836/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G829/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/H026/>;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFSN0777";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "sea_surface_height_above_reference_ellipsoid"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/ULAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/JV4KKW4W/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
